WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.160
Good morning John, it's Friday.

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:03.240
You're an author, you love books, I love books
too.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.920
I read a lot of 'em, though apparently I am
mildly dyslexic enough that I read at the

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.730
level of third grader according to this Staples
test.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.360
But listen, we've been making books for thousands
of years now, so you'd think that we'd've

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:19.900
perfected the process, and yet I HAVE SOME
THINGS that I would like to discuss with publishing

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.900
companies.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.810
So I love to spend time at bookstores, and
to figure out what I want, I read the reviews,

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.720
and I read the blurbs, and also I read the
description.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.730
WHY would you put a major spoiler in the description
on the back of the book?!

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:34.611
I don't want to be halfway through a book
and the thing that I know is going to happen

00:00:34.611 --> 00:00:38.170
hasn't happened yet 'cause I read it on the
thing that's like two paragraphs!

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:41.000
Is it so hard to keep a spoiler out of that?!

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.230
Why would you do that!?

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.230
Interestingly, Looking for Alaska does not
do this, but, in one edition that I noticed—so

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.400
there's the author bio on the back page, and
it's like "Looking for Alaska was written

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.470
by John Green, who likes to make candy canes
and lollipops and lives with his puppy in

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.860
Indianapolis"—that's what it says, right?—if
you turn to that page to read about the author,

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:01.490
which you might want to do before you read
the book, on the facing page there's a, like

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:06.500
a question for, you know, reader questions
for like if it's taught in a school or something,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:12.530
and the—the question is: "Do you think that
SPOILER SPOILER was a SPOILER or a SPOILER?"

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:13.650
Think about this stuff!

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.740
Okay, also, if a book is part of a series,
please just let me know.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.360
On the cover, or the inside flap, just like
five words.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.659
"This is the second book in the Sword of Butts
trilogy," so I KNOW.

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:30.140
Do it like graphic novels do it, just put
a little number on the bottom that—it so—it's

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:31.140
great.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.680
There's nothing wrong with EXCEPT WHEN YOU
DO THIS!

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.340
NEVER, EVER!

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.200
THAT'S THE WORST THING ON MY BOOKSHELF!

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.331
They're all first editions!

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:40.310
It's not even from a different edition!

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.840
They just made a mistake!

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.080
And I hate it so much!

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.080
Anyway, covers.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.040
You can't judge a book by 'em, but don't,
if it's like a really high quality fantasy

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.880
epic, I don't want to see a half-naked guy
on a horse with a sword.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.360
I'm not gonna believe that it's a good book
if there's a half-naked guy on a sword with

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.360
a horse!

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.360
On a sword with a horse?

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.360
I take it back.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.979
If there's a half-naked guy on a sword, then
I'll probably buy that book.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.100
Decapitated YA fiction ladies?

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.100
Stop.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.100
We've had enough.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:08.790
Books that look like they're Twilight but
aren't Twilight?

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:10.650
No, why would you do that?

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.190
Okay, here's another thing.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:15.180
This is the, The Ocean at the End of the Lane,
amazing book.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.280
This — I don't know if you can see this,
let, let's get in here.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.740
This is the, your deckled edge, where it's
not, it's not, uh, flat.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.740
Why?

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.740
Why?

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:25.740
Why?

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:26.820
This makes it harder to read the book!

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.420
And it's always in books that are, like, fancier
and more expensive.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.530
So it must cost more.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.921
If you're paying more for something that makes
something less functional, maybe not do it.

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:40.470
It's like during those years when pants got
really expensive if they had pre-made holes

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.510
in them.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.280
I can make my own pant holes, thank you.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.650
Also, just don't lie to me.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.080
This is The Casual Vacancy by J.K.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.960
Rowling, and it says on the back "The big
novel about a small town that became a #1

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:54.960
worldwide bestseller."

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.680
That is a little misleading.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.650
"It overcame insurmountable odds to become
this popular book!"

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:07.400
It's written by the eleventh most popular
author of all time.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.400
It might become a number one bestseller.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.950
I don't think anybody was surprised.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.840
Books are nice, but they're not a perfect
format.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.160
I mean, you have to charge the battery all
the time, there's all those annoying pop-up

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.340
advertisements, pre-rolls.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:24.040
What I'm basically saying is THIS IS A FAR
SUPERIOR MEDIA TO MOST OF THEM.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.320
Except for Vlogbrothers videos, which are
the best of all the media.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.700
By pinnacling it!

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:32.160
This is the Cadillac of the ultimate!

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.400
Not penultimate, which does not mean the same
thing, which I learned while I was giving

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.150
an oral presentation and somebody laughed
at me.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.160
So.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.730
John, I'll see you on Tuesday!

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.550
The first episode of The Art Assignment is.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.550
On.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:49.550
Line.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:50.550
Right.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:52.250
Now.!Go to youtube.com/theartassignment and
watch it.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.450
The Yeti has a Youtube channel.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.660
Who would have guessed?

